  "narrative": "During the 1980s, Black and African women's hairstyles and hair culture were deeply intertwined with their identity and sense of community. The Jheri curl, a permanent wave hairstyle invented by Jheri Redding, was a popular trend among Black Americans during this decade. This style gave wearers a glossy, loosely curled look that was touted as easier to care for than other chemical treatments of the time. While there is limited documentation on hairstyles and hair culture specifically from this era, it's clear that protective styles such as box braids and cornrows were still revered for their cultural significance and ability to minimize breakage in Afro-textured hair. These traditional hairstyles continued to be an essential part of African women's identity and expression, reflecting a deep connection to their heritage and community.",
